## Quick Answer

If your Seattle remodel only changes what you see, such as paint, cabinets, counters or insulation, you do not need a remodel permit. If it changes what holds the house up or how you get out of a room, such as opening a wall, enlarging a window or finishing a basement, you do. Small jobs worth **$6,000 or less in any six months** are exempt, but that break never covers a load-bearing wall, the outer shell of the house or your way out in a fire.

Most house remodels use a fast-lane permit called **Subject-to-Field-Inspection (STFI)**, which the city screens in a few days. Bigger structural work goes through full plan review, which right now means about two months of city time and roughly four months on your calendar. You will deal with two offices: SDCI, the Seattle Department of Construction and Inspections, for construction and electrical permits, and Public Health - Seattle & King County for plumbing.

## Start With Your Project, Not the Code

Describe your remodel the way you would to a neighbor and the permit list follows. Three common projects cover most of the questions.

### A kitchen refresh that stays in its footprint

You are replacing cabinets, counters, tile, flooring and paint, and the sink and range stay put. Cabinets, paint and new surface finishes over existing walls and ceilings are exempt by name, so none of that needs a construction permit; flooring is not on that list, so it rides on the $6,000 rule explained below.

One detail changes the picture. If the island gets a new circuit, or any wiring is added or moved, you need an electrical permit even without a construction permit, so a cosmetic kitchen with one new circuit still has one permit on the job. And the moment a wall opens, a window grows or the range moves to another wall, the construction permit comes back. Layout options are on our [kitchen remodel](/services/kitchen-remodel) page.

### A bathroom where the toilet moves

A [bathroom remodel](/services/bathroom-remodel) that swaps fixtures in place is finish work and follows the kitchen logic. The moment the toilet, the shower or the sink moves, the drain lines move with it, and that paperwork goes to Public Health - Seattle & King County, not SDCI. New or rerouted wiring adds the electrical permit, and taking out a wall, even a non-bearing one, is an alteration that falls under the $6,000 rule rather than the exempt-by-name list. So a bathroom with a moved toilet carries a plumbing permit at minimum before the construction permit question even comes up.

### A basement you want to finish

This one is almost never exempt. Finishing a basement creates habitable rooms, changes how you get out in an emergency and nearly always adds circuits and plumbing, so plan on a construction permit, an electrical permit and, if a bathroom goes in, a plumbing permit. A basement bedroom needs a window big enough to climb through, which is why the word egress comes up in every basement conversation. Our [basement finishing](/services/basement-finishing) page covers the layout side.

Anything that adds floor area beyond a small single-story addition, or creates a new dwelling unit, leaves the fast lane entirely.

## The $6,000 Rule in Plain Words

You can skip the construction permit for minor repairs and alterations that cost $6,000 or less in any six-month period, according to [the city's exemption rules](https://www.seattle.gov/construction-and-inspections/permits/do-you-need-a-permit). Two details trip homeowners up. The city counts fair market value of labor and parts even when you do the work yourself, so your weekends count at what a contractor would have charged, and three small invoices inside the same six months add up to one project.

Three exceptions override the dollar figure no matter how small the job:

- **Load-bearing supports.** The walls, posts and beams holding up the floor or roof above you. Removing, shortening or moving one needs a permit even if it costs $2,000.
- **The building envelope.** The shell between inside and outside: exterior walls, roof, foundation, windows and doors. Enlarging a window opening or cutting a new door changes it.
- **Egress, light, ventilation and fire resistance.** Egress is the way out of a room in an emergency: the doors, the hallway to them and, in a bedroom or basement, a window big enough to climb through. Blocking or shrinking that path, or reducing light, ventilation or fire resistance, is never exempt.

Back to the three projects. The kitchen refresh passes as long as no wall opens and no window grows. The bathroom passes until the job tops $6,000 or touches a bearing wall. The basement fails on egress and habitable rooms before you even reach the dollar figure.

Two more things. Every exempt project still has to meet code; the exemption removes the paperwork, not the rules. And on a lot in an environmentally critical area, such as a steep slope, the usual exemptions may not apply.

## Who You Will Deal With

Two offices, not one. Your construction, electrical and any mechanical permit for heating, ventilation and air conditioning all come from SDCI, each with its own inspections separate from the construction permit ([the city's inspection rules](https://www.seattle.gov/construction-and-inspections/inspections/construction-inspections)). Seattle runs its own electrical code, and you need the electrical permit any time wiring is installed, altered, extended or connected to equipment ([electrical permit rules](https://www.seattle.gov/construction-and-inspections/permits/permits-we-issue-%28a-z%29/electrical-permit)). The electrician who pulls it needs a Washington State electrical contractor's license and a Seattle business license; if you own and live in the house, you may do your own wiring under that permit.

Plumbing is the odd one out: moving a sink, a toilet or a drain means a permit from Public Health - Seattle & King County, which you coordinate with directly. For your quote, the lesson is that trade permits exist even when the construction permit does not, so a good estimate names every one.

## STFI: The Fast Lane Most Remodels Use

Remodeling a portion of your house, a detached garage and a small single-story addition are the city's own examples of projects that qualify for a Subject-to-Field-Inspection permit, a construction permit for small projects with fewer plan requirements because the work is simple ([STFI permit requirements](https://www.seattle.gov/construction-and-inspections/permits/permits-we-issue-%28a-z%29/construction-permit---subject-to-field-inspection)). Instead of a plan reviewer checking every detail on paper first, the building inspector checks the code requirements on site during the field inspections, and your permit is issued subject to what the inspector finds.

What you upload: a Building & Land Use Pre-Application in the Seattle Services Portal (services.seattle.gov) with a site plan and the legal description of your lot, then drawings to scale, easy to read, showing both existing conditions and proposed changes. The city checks that the project qualifies and complies with the land use code, may ask for more information, and issues the permit and stamped plans electronically once fees are paid. That screening takes a few days; if your project is bigger than STFI allows, the city tells you how to apply for a regular permit instead.

Structure and scale push a remodel out of the fast lane. A new beam or header in a load-bearing wall can still qualify when its span stays within the city's limits; longer spans need an engineer's stamp, and the limits are in our guide to a [main floor remodel in Seattle](/blog/main-floor-remodel-cost-seattle). Disturbing more than 750 square feet of land moves you to an Addition or Alteration permit.

## Full Plan Review: How Long a Seattle Remodel Permit Takes

Expanding or renovating beyond what STFI covers puts your project in the single-family addition and alteration category, which moves in review cycles: the city sends a correction notice, your designer updates the drawings and resubmits, and the cycle repeats until every issue is resolved. The city's clock pauses whenever the application is back with you, which is why your total wait runs longer than its own figure.

Here is what that means for your calendar. Since October 1, 2025 the city has set review goals by project type; for a single-family addition or alteration the goal is 30 calendar days in city control, and the current result is 64 days at the 75th percentile, meaning three out of four permits are issued faster than that ([the city's permit performance page](https://www.seattle.gov/construction-and-inspections/about-us/construction-permit-performance)). The total time a homeowner experiences is roughly twice that city-control number, because it adds the days your designer spends answering correction notices. For budgeting, plan on about four months from intake to permit for a remodel with structural changes, with a fair chance of less.

A complete application at intake and a fast reply to every correction notice shorten that wait most. Once issued, your construction permit is valid for 18 months, with a renewal if the project runs longer. Electrical permits move faster: many are issued online the same day you apply, and one that needs plan review may take up to a few weeks when no corrections are needed.

## What Happens on Site During Inspections

Once the permit is issued, the inspector becomes part of your project. The permit holder requests each inspection, and the inspector checks the work against the approved plans and the building code. For a remodel the usual stops are framing, before anything is insulated or covered; insulation, once it is installed; and final, after all construction is complete and every related permit has had its own final.

Your electrician has three stops of their own: a cover inspection before the walls close, a service inspection and a final. That order shapes your calendar: no drywall goes up until the framing, the wiring and the plumbing inside that wall have each been signed off.

Scheduling is quick. Your contractor requests the inspection online or through the 24-hour automated line; a request in before 7 a.m. is usually inspected the same day, and later requests go to the next workday. Keep the permit displayed where it can be seen and the stamped plans on site at every inspection. After the final inspection the city grants approval to occupy the space.

## What Skipping the Permit Costs You

Start a building, addition or remodeling project without a permit and the city may issue a stop work order; build without one or skip the final inspection approval and you face a fine of up to $500 per day or other enforcement action ([the city's enforcement rules](https://www.seattle.gov/construction-and-inspections/codes/common-code-questions/construction-without-a-permit)). The city's own reasons to permit are really three risks of skipping it: resale value, insurance coverage of repairs, and complaints about unpermitted work.

Resale is where unpermitted remodels usually surface. A buyer's inspector or lender compares the house with the permit record, and a finished basement or a moved wall with nothing behind it becomes a negotiation point or a loan condition.

If you find old unpermitted work during your own remodel, do not build over it. Raise it with SDCI before the next inspection: the inspector will see it anyway, and the fix is cheaper while the wall is open. The city offers 20 minutes of free coaching with a permit specialist; bring the address, a sketch of the existing structures with dimensions and distances to the property lines, and a description of what you plan to do.

## Before You Apply

- **Look up the address.** The Seattle Services Portal lists recent permits and any violations; the city's Research a Project tool covers older history. A third bathroom that no permit ever mentions is worth knowing about before you hand drawings to the city.
- **Gather the paperwork.** The address and full legal description of the lot, a site plan, drawings to scale of existing conditions and proposed changes, and the completed STFI checklist for single-family and duplex buildings.
- **Budget for fees as their own line.** Permit fees are based on the value of the project and collected mostly at intake, so there is no flat price list; ask your contractor to show them separately in the quote.
- **File everything together.** The electrical and plumbing permits go in with the construction permit, not after framing starts.

## Frequently Asked Questions

### Do I need a permit to remodel a kitchen in Seattle?

Replacing cabinets, counters, tile and paint does not need a construction permit, and the $6,000 minor-work exemption covers other small changes. Moving a wall, enlarging a window or changing the layout does. New or moved wiring needs an electrical permit and moved plumbing needs a Public Health permit either way.

### Can I pull my own permits as a homeowner?

For electrical work, yes: property owners may do their own wiring on a house they own and occupy. The construction permit application runs through the Seattle Services Portal; make sure your quote says who files it and who schedules the inspections.

### What happens if the previous owner remodeled without a permit?

The exposure moves to you once you own the house: the fine for work done without a permit or a final inspection approval runs up to $500 per day, and an inspector on your new permit will see the old work once the walls are open. Check the Seattle Services Portal for the address before you buy, and again before you remodel the same space.

